OpenSSL 4.1.0: an ECH rejection after a HelloRetryRequest ends with illegal_parameter

Carried by Kuicksilver under ADR-0019 (docs/adr/0019-openssl-4-and-ech.md), the second of the two carried patches (applied
after openssl-4.1.0-ech-hrr-custom-ext.patch); pinned by its SHA-256 in gradle/openssl.toml as patch_2, applied by
tools/openssl/apply-patch.pl to the verified tarball, and dropped with the first OpenSSL release that fixes the bug.

The bug: tls_process_server_hello (ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c) aborts with illegal_parameter when the ServerHello does not
confirm ECH and the HelloRetryRequest before it carried an encrypted_client_hello extension (hrrsignal_p != NULL), taking
the extension's presence for an acceptance, where RFC 9849 section 6.1.4 has the client compare its payload. A server that rejects ECH may send that extension with eight random octets
(RFC 9849 section 7.1; OpenSSL's own server does, extensions_srvr.c tls_construct_stoc_ech), so a client whose ECH a
server rejects after a HelloRetryRequest never reaches the rejection path of RFC 9849 section 6.1.6 (authenticate the
public_name, ech_required, retry_configs). A second issue on the same path: the fallback to the public_name runs at the
HelloRetryRequest and again at the ServerHello, and the second time overwrites former_inner (the real server name) with
the public_name, leaking the first string.

The fix: remember whether the HelloRetryRequest's confirmation matched (success == 1 then) before the ServerHello's check
overwrites it, and abort only when it did; fall back to the public_name only once.

--- a/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
+++ b/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
@@ -1951,6 +1951,15 @@
         && s->ext.ech.done != 1 && s->ext.ech.ch_depth == 0
         && s->ext.ech.grease == OSSL_ECH_NOT_GREASE
         && s->ext.ech.attempted_type == TLSEXT_TYPE_ech) {
+        /*
+         * Whether a HelloRetryRequest accepted ECH, before the ServerHello's
+         * check below overwrites the result: a rejecting server may send an
+         * ECH extension with random octets in its HelloRetryRequest, so its
+         * presence alone is no acceptance.
+         */
+        int hrr_accepted = !hrr && s->ext.ech.hrrsignal_p != NULL
+            && s->ext.ech.success == 1;
+
         if (!set_client_ciphersuite(s, cipherchars)) {
             /* SSLfatal() already called */
             goto err;
@@ -2007,8 +2016,7 @@
              * the outer CH, so we better set the hostname for the
              * connection accordingly.
              */
-        } else if (!hrr && s->ext.ech.success == 0
-            && s->ext.ech.hrrsignal_p != NULL) {
+        } else if (!hrr && s->ext.ech.success == 0 && hrr_accepted) {
             /*
              * we previously saw a good HRR ECH acceptance but now
              * the SH.random ECH acceptance signal is bad so that's an
@@ -2016,7 +2024,8 @@
              */
             SSLfatal(s, SSL_AD_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER, SSL_R_ECH_REQUIRED);
             goto err;
-        } else {
+        } else if (s->ext.ech.former_inner == NULL) {
+            /* once: after a rejecting HelloRetryRequest it was done already */
             OSSL_TRACE1(TLS, "ECH falling back to public_name: %s\n",
                 s->ext.ech.outer_hostname != NULL ? s->ext.ech.outer_hostname : "NONE");
             s->ext.ech.former_inner = s->ext.hostname;
